rt job or have a cron\njob to remove xpaccel log files periodically. Something like\n-bash-4.2# cat /etc/cron.daily/xpaccel-cleanup.sh\n#!/bin/bash\nrm -f /var/log/agents/xpaccel-[0-9]*.gz\n-bash-4.2# ls -la /etc/cron.daily/xpaccel-cleanup.sh\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 root root 52 Apr  2 09:11 /etc/cron.daily/xpaccel-cleanup.sh\n-bash-4.2#\nCustomer can create above file and also add create this file from /mnt/flash/rc.eos.\n\n', 'product': 'eos', 'versionIntroduced': ['4.18.1'], 'versionFixed': ['4.24.11', '4.25.9', '4.26.7', '4.27.nofixyet', '4.28.nofixyet', '4.29.nofixyet', '4.30.nofixyet', '4.31.nofixyet', '4.32.nofixyet', '4.33.nofixyet', '4.34.nofixyet', '4.35.nofixyet', '4.36.nofixyet', '4.37.nofixyet'], 'bites': 2, 'lastBiteTime': 1647299740, 'exportedTime': 1700784000, 'releaseNote': 'After a long uptime, sometimes "show tech-support" may encounter SYS-4-CLI_SCHEDULER_ABORT log message.\nThis is caused by logfiles of xpaccel growing in size overtime.\n\nThe workaround is to increase the timeout for the scheduled tech-support job or have a cron\njob to remove xpaccel log files periodically. Something like\n-bash-4.2# cat /etc/cron.daily/xpaccel-cleanup.sh\n#!/bin/bash\nrm -f /var/log/agents/xpaccel-[0-9]*.gz\n-bash-4.2# ls -la /etc/cron.daily/xpaccel-cleanup.sh\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 root root 52 Apr  2 09:11 /etc/cron.daily/xpaccel-cleanup.sh\n-bash-4.2#\nCustomer can create above file and also add create this file from /mnt/flash/rc.eos.\n\n', 'conjunction': [[{'tag': '7160'}]]}, {'bugId': 680577, 'severity': 'sev2', 'securityAdvisoryURL': '', 'cve': '', 'alertSummary': 'Committing CLI config session containing "hardware access-list update default-result permit" command and router access-list updates may result in traffic loss for the VLAN corresponding to router access-list.', 'alertNote': 'Committing a CLI config session containing "hardware access-list update default-result permit" command and router access-list updates may result in permanent loss of all data plane and con