apidly between the VTEPs. Refer to the bug release notes for more details', 'alertNote': 'If a MAC entry ends up on a device with an erroneously large last move time, and then that MAC moves to a remote VTEP in an EVPN/VXLAN topology, the MAC may flap back and forth rapidly between the moved-from and the moved-to VTEP until EVPN host-flap detection triggers and removes the MAC advertisements from the control plane.  The flapping may resume when the suppression of the MAC expires and the MAC is re-advertised into the control plane, leading to repeated suppression of the MAC from EVPN.\n\nOne known cause of erroneously large last move times is when an MLAG device is upgraded via SSU.\n\nAs a workaround, the MAC entries with erroneously large last move times should be manually cleared.  They should not recur unless the original trigger for the separate bug that caused them is repeated.', 'product': 'eos', 'versionIntroduced': ['1.0.0'], 'versionFixed': ['4.33.10', '4.34.8', '4.35.6', '4.36.3', '4.37.0'], 'bites': 1, 'lastBiteTime': 1780190482, 'exportedTime': 1791244800, 'releaseNote': 'If a MAC entry ends up on a device with an erroneously large last move time, and then that MAC moves to a remote VTEP in an EVPN/VXLAN topology, the MAC may flap back and forth rapidly between the moved-from and the moved-to VTEP until EVPN host-flap detection triggers and removes the MAC advertisements from the control plane.  The flapping may resume when the suppression of the MAC expires and the MAC is re-advertised into the control plane, leading to repeated suppression of the MAC from EVPN.\n\nOne known cause of erroneously large last move times is when an MLAG device is upgraded via SSU.\n\nAs a workaround, the MAC entries with erroneously large last move times should be manually cleared.  They should not recur unless the original trigger for the separate bug that caused them is repeated.', 'conjunction': [[{'tag': 'evpnEnabled'}, {'tag': 'mlagEnabled'}, {'tag': 'vxlanEnabled'}]]}